{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJW95cpCA89UmCocUc5Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xbvnu4NdNu7ycVubvsgxvh","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work; cat > answer.md <<'E'\n# Answer\n\n1. A stable set exists **iff K is even**.\n2. For K = 2m the stable set is **unique**: V = {(m,m,0), (m,0,m), (0,m,m)}. For odd K there is none.\n(Confirmed by exhaustive search K=2..11, `bf.py`.)\n\n## Proofs\nNotation: x dominates y iff x_i>y_i and x_j>y_j for some i≠j.\n\n**Lemma 1 (internal stability).** Distinct u,v (same sum K) have no domination either way iff they agree in some coordinate.\nIf u_a=v_a, the other two differences sum to 0 and are nonzero, so one is positive, one negative: u beats v in at most one other coordinate. Neither dominates. If no coordinate agrees, the three nonzero differences u_i−v_i sum to 0, so two share a sign, and u or v dominates the other.\nSo V is internally stable iff any two elements agree in some coordinate.\n\n**Lemma 2.** If x_i = y_i, x cannot dominate y via any pair containing i; and x with x_j+x_k = y_j+y_k cannot dominate y via {j,k}. Hence if x_i=y_i then x does not dominate y.\n\n**Existence, K=2m.** V is internally stable by Lemma 1 (any two share a 'm' or '0'… e.g. (m,m,0),(m,0,m) share coordinate 1). Externally: y is undominated by (0,m,m) iff y_2≥m or y_3≥m, etc. y is undominated by all three iff at least two coordinates are ≥m (each of the three tests is \"some of two specific coordinates ≥m\", i.e. y_1≥m or y_2≥m ... together at least two coordinates ≥ m: if only y_1≥m, then (m,m,0)... y_2,y_3<m is dominated by (0,m,m)). Precisely: y is dominated by (0,m,m) iff y_2<m,y_3<m. So y undominated by all iff no two coordinates are both <m, i.e. at least two coords ≥m; sum 2m forces those two to equal m and the third 0, i.e. y∈V. So V is stable.\n\n**Classification.** Let V be stable. V≠∅ (otherwise nothing is dominated). Call V *centered* if all elements share the same value in the same position.\n\n*Case A: centered*, say x_1=c for all x∈V. Take y with y_1=c+1 if c<K (else y=(0,K,0) when c=K, V={(K,0,0)} and only... then V=L_c={(K,0,0)} and y=(0,K,0) is dominated by nothing in V, since (K,0,0) beats it only in coordinate 1).\nFor c<K: y=(c+1,·,·) is not in V; x∈V has x_1=c<y_1 so a dominating pair must be {2,3}: x_2>y_2,x_3>y_3 gives x_2+x_3≥y_2+y_3+2 = K−c−1+2 > K−c = x_2+x_3, contradiction. So y undominated, y∉V: contradiction. Case A impossible.\n\n*Case B: not centered.* Then |V|≥2; take u≠v in V, agreeing (Lemma 1) in position a, WLOG a=1: u=(a,b,α), v=(a,β,e), b≠β, α≠e. Since V isn't centered at (1,a), some w∈V has w_1≠a. w agrees with u in position 2 or 3, with v likewise; if both in the same position, u,v would agree there and in position 1, so u=v. So WLOG (relabel 2↔3 swapping u,v roles) w agrees with u in position 2 and with v in position 3: w=(γ,b,e), γ=K−b−e. Here α=K−a−b, β=K−a−e, γ=K−b−e, and γ≠a (else w... w∈{u,v}? if γ=a, w=(a,b,e) agrees with u in 1,2 so w=u, but w_3=e≠α) — indeed γ−a=β−b=α−e=:δ=K−a−b−e≠0.\nClaim V={u,v,w}. Let z∈V, z∉{u,v,w}. If z_1=a: agreeing with w needs z_2=b (z=u) or z_3=e (z=v) (z_1=γ=a impossible). So z_1≠a. Agreeing with u and v then needs positions 2,3: (z_2=b or z_3=α) and (z_2=β or z_3=e); as b≠β, α≠e: either z_2=b,z_3=e (z=w), or z_3=α,z_2=β, z=(K−α−β,β,α). Agreeing with w then forces z_1=γ (z_2=β≠b, z_3=α≠e), i.e. K−α−β=γ, i.e. K=α+β+γ = 3K−2(a+b+e)... use α+β+γ=3K−2(a+b+e); so a+b+e = K, δ=0, contradiction. So |V|=3: the \"triangle\" u=L1∩L2, v=L1∩L3, w=L2∩L3, lines L1:{x_1=a}, L2:{x_2=b}, L3:{x_3=e}.\n\n*External stability on the lines.* Take y on L1 (y_1=a), y∉V. By Lemma 2, u,v don't dominate y, so w must: two of y_1<γ, y_2<b, y_3<e. Write y=(a,t,K−a−t), t∈[0,K−a]; y_3<e ⇔ t>β.\n(i) If δ<0 (γ<a): y_1<γ fails, so need β<t<b for every t∈[0,K−a]\\{b,β}. Hence b>β, and t=0 forces β=0 (if β>0, 0∉{b,β}... 0 is not in (β,b)), t=K−a forces b=K−a. So u,v are the two endpoints (a,K−a,0),(a,0,K−a) of segment L1. The hypothesis and conclusion are symmetric under permuting players (δ and the triangle are symmetric), so the same holds for L2 and L3: the two vertices on each line are its two endpoints. Take u=(a,K−a,0), v=(a,0,K−a) (the other order is the swap 2↔3). Then b=K−a, e=K−a; w=(γ,K−a,K−a) is an endpoint of L2={x_2=K−a}, so w∈{(0,K−a,a),(a,K−a,0)=u}; w≠u so e=a, thus a=K−a, K=2a is even and V={(a,a,0),(a,0,a),(0,a,a)}, the claimed set.\n(ii) If δ>0 then b<β (β−b=δ). Points t strictly between b and β are not in V, are not in {t<b} nor {t>β}, and y_1<γ with y_2<b or y_3<e fails for them, and the pair (b>t>β) fails; so none exist: β=b+1, δ=1, i.e. a+b+e=K−1, and V={(a,b,e+1),(a,b+1,e),(a+1,b,e)}. Since K≥2, (a,b,e)≠(0,0,0). If e≥1 let y=(a+1,b+1,e−1); if b≥1, y=(a+1,b−1,e+1); if a≥1, y=(a−1,b+1,e+1). In each case y is ≥ each of u,v,w in two coordinates (check directly), so no element of V dominates y, and y∉V: contradiction. So (ii) is impossible.\n\nHence every stable set is {(m,m,0),(m,0,m),(0,m,m)} with K=2m; for odd K none exists. ∎\nE\necho \"[sonnet] 05:00 wrote full proof+answer in answer.md (even K unique, odd none; brute-force agrees to K=11). FINAL\" >> board.md; cat board.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12598,"cache_read_input_tokens":18820,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12598,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5325cffa-b096-4459-af67-2b0365b902a1","uuid":"32bfe2c2-50e1-4cc0-8760-44911897da2d","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:02:27.316Z","request_id":"req_011CgJW95GF7sLiCEdx2LdH6"}
